sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To inscribe a square in a given circle.
----
proof:

Let {ABCD} be the given circle.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="300" height="300" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="IV.6 diagram" src="propIV6.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "IV.6",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;145,30",
            "C;point;free;145,260",
            "E;point;midpoint;A,C;black;green",
            "ABCD;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;black;random",
            "B;point;perpendicular;E,A",
            "D;point;perpendicular;E,C",
            "square;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,C,D;0;0;black;random",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            // Semicircle BAD as an invisible highlight target. The two radii
            // ED and EB are collinear — together they are the diameter BD —
            // so the wedge is exactly the semicircle, not an approximation of
            // it. Arm order D,B picks the half containing A (euclid#172:
            // arm order chooses the side).
            "BAD;sector;sector;E,D,B;0;0;0;0",
            // Invisible highlight targets: the proof argues over the four
            // joined sides and the three radii by name, but the figure drew
            // only the quadrilateral and the two diameters.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;0",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D;0;0;0",
            "DA;line;connect;D,A;0;0;0",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E;0;0;0",
            "ED;line;connect;E,D;0;0;0",
            "EA;line;connect;E,A;0;0;0",
            "angAEB;sector;angleMarker;E,A,B;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angBAD;sector;angleMarker;A,B,D;0;0;orange;orange"
        ],
        // A and C are the circle's free points, so without deferring them the
        // auto-union would stand them on slides 1-2 with labels the caption has
        // not reached — and only two of the four points the name ABCD implies.
        // The construction introduces all four with the diameters, in slide 3.
        deferDraggables: ["A", "C"],
        // Joyce writes AD for the side the figure declares DA; the circle and
        // the inscribed quadrilateral are both named ABCD in the prose, so the
        // quadrilateral is reached with the display override {ABCD|square}.
        aliases: {
            "AD": "DA"
        },
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {ABCD} be the given circle.",
              visible: ["ABCD"],
              highlighted: ["ABCD"] },
            { text: "It is required to inscribe a square in the circle {ABCD}.",
              visible: ["ABCD"],
              highlighted: ["ABCD"] },
            { text: "Draw two diameters {AC} and {BD} of the circle {ABCD} at right angles to one another, and join {AB}, {BC}, {CD}, and {DA}.",
              visible: ["ABCD", "AC", "BD", "A", "B", "C", "D", "AB", "BC", "CD", "DA", "square"],
              highlighted: ["AC", "BD", "AB", "BC", "CD", "DA"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "AC",     name: "Line.straightEdgeConnect" },
                  { elem: "BD",     name: "Line.straightEdgeConnect" },
                  // The four joins are drawn by the quadrilateral: AB, BC, CD
                  // and DA are zero-colour highlight targets, so animating
                  // them individually would draw nothing. Outlining `square`
                  // traces the same four sides, in order.
                  { elem: "square", name: "Polygon.outline" } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "a diameter through the centre", ref: "III.1" },
                  { claim: "at right angles to one another", ref: "I.11" } ] },
            { text: "Then, since {BE} equals {ED}, for {E} is the center, and {EA} is common and at right angles, therefore the base {AB} equals the base {AD}.",
              visible: ["ABCD", "AC", "BD", "A", "B", "C", "D", "AB", "BC", "CD", "DA", "square", "E", "BE", "ED", "EA", "angAEB"],
              highlighted: ["BE", "ED", "EA", "angAEB", "AB", "DA"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angAEB", name: "Sector.sweep" } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "two sides and the included angle", ref: "I.4" }] },
            { text: "For the same reason each of the straight lines {BC} and {CD} also equals each of the straight lines {AB} and {AD}. Therefore the quadrilateral {ABCD|square} is equilateral.",
              visible: ["ABCD", "AC", "BD", "A", "B", "C", "D", "AB", "BC", "CD", "DA", "E", "square"],
              highlighted: ["AB", "BC", "CD", "DA"] },
            { text: "I say next that it is also right-angled.",
              highlighted: ["square"] },
            { text: "For, since the straight line {BD} is a diameter of the circle {ABCD}, therefore {BAD} is a semicircle, therefore the angle {BAD|angBAD} is right.",
              visible: ["ABCD", "AC", "BD", "A", "B", "C", "D", "AB", "BC", "CD", "DA", "E", "square", "BAD", "angBAD"],
              highlighted: ["BD", "BAD", "angBAD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "BAD",    name: "Sector.sweep" },
                  { elem: "angBAD", name: "Sector.sweep" } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "an angle in a semicircle is right", ref: "III.31" }] },
            { text: "For the same reason each of the angles ABC, BCD, and CDA is also right. Therefore the quadrilateral {ABCD|square} is right-angled.",
              visible: ["ABCD", "AC", "BD", "A", "B", "C", "D", "AB", "BC", "CD", "DA", "E", "square"],
              highlighted: ["square"] },
            { text: "But it was also proved equilateral, therefore it is a square, and it has been inscribed in the circle {ABCD}. Therefore the square {ABCD|square} has been inscribed in the given circle.",
              highlighted: ["square", "ABCD"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.4":    "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI4/",
        "I.11":   "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI11/",
        "III.1":  "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII1/",
        "III.31": "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII31/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

It is required to inscribe a square in the circle {ABCD}.

Draw two diameters {AC} and {BD} of the circle {ABCD} at right angles to one another, and join {AB}, {BC}, {CD}, and {DA}. [!just III.1, I.11]

Then, since {BE} equals {ED}, for {E} is the center, and {EA} is common and at right angles, therefore the base {AB} equals the base {AD}. [!just I.4]

For the same reason each of the straight lines {BC} and {CD} also equals each of the straight lines {AB} and {AD}. Therefore the quadrilateral {ABCD|square} is equilateral.

I say next that it is also right-angled.

For, since the straight line {BD} is a diameter of the circle {ABCD}, therefore {BAD} is a semicircle, therefore the angle {BAD|angBAD} is right. [!just III.31]

For the same reason each of the angles *ABC, BCD,* and *CDA* is also right. Therefore the quadrilateral {ABCD|square} is right-angled.

But it was also proved equilateral, therefore it is a square, and it has been inscribed in the circle {ABCD}.

Therefore the square {ABCD|square} has been inscribed in the given circle.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
<br clear="all">
